  "abstract": "A rational Diophantine tuple has pairwise products plus one square, and an entry is strong when its square plus one is square. We prove that five specified coordinate functions in the elliptic square-product quintuple families of Dujella, Kazalicki and Petričević are never strong at a valid rational parameter: coordinate D in family 1, coordinates A and D in family 2, and B and E in family 3. These exclusions complement an earlier exclusion of family 3 coordinate C. The proof combines a finite square-class descent with five explicit genus-two quotient curves. Exact 2-descent certificates give rank-one Jacobians, and exact Mumford arithmetic determines the reduction of a Chabauty annihilating differential at the prime 5. For two quotient curves we determine every rational point. For the other three, the original square conditions restrict the eligible residue discs, each of which is occupied by a known degenerate or incompatible point. The rank bounds use unconditionally certified number-field calculations and necessary local Hilbert-symbol equations; the proof uses no analytic-rank assumption or finite-search extrapolation. A standalone supplement reproduces the coordinate identities, rank bounds, finite-field orders, non-torsion checks, and local logarithm data. The results exclude six of the fifteen coordinate families when combined with the earlier work. They restrict a specific route toward tuples containing a product-minus-one pair; they do not settle the existence of arbitrary strong square-product quintuples, square-product sextuples, or rational Diophantine septuples.",
